usage: order_book_cli replay <path>... [--trades] [--events] [--book] [--validate none|final|per-command]
                                       [--symbol SYM] [--no-statistics]
       order_book_cli convert <path>... --out <path> [--symbol SYM]
       order_book_cli send <file> --connect ADDR:PORT [--symbol SYM]
       order_book_cli checkpoint describe|verify <file>
       order_book_cli checkpoint roll --log-dir DIR [--checkpoint-dir DIR]
       order_book_cli verify --log-dir DIR [--checkpoint-dir DIR] [--validate final|per-command]
       order_book_cli cut-log --log-dir DIR --shard S --position P [--checkpoint-dir DIR]
       order_book_cli demo
       order_book_cli --help

Replays a CSV command file (docs/replay-format.md) or a binary command log
(docs/command-log-format.md), told apart by its first bytes, on an empty order book and
prints replay statistics. Never prompts: everything is set on the command line.

commands:
  replay <path>...   replay the input, then print its statistics; a directory means every
                     log file in it
  convert <path>...  turn a CSV into a log, or a log into a v3 CSV; several inputs or a
                     directory make --out a directory of commands-<symbol>.csv
  send <file>        send the file's commands to a running order_book_engine and print
                     its answers as replay --events --no-statistics prints the file
  checkpoint describe <file>
                     print a checkpoint file's two headers (docs/checkpoint-format.md),
                     checking nothing past its first bytes
  checkpoint verify <file>
                     check a checkpoint file's checksum, then rebuild its book from the
                     image; print ok, or the first fault's byte offset, code, and message
  checkpoint roll    roll an engine's checkpoints forward over its closed log files, as its
                     workers do, with no engine running; print the files and books rolled
  verify             what an engine's start would make of its log and checkpoints, changing
                     nothing: a line per shard (where its replay starts, its log ends, and
                     what a start would drop) and per book, then ok
  cut-log            move a shard's record at a position, and every one after it, to a
                     cut- file beside the log, keeping every byte, so a start passes them
  demo               print the events of a short scripted session
  help               print this help

replay options, in any order before or after the file:
  --trades           print each trade, tagged with its command's line, before the statistics
  --events           print every event instead, tagged with its command's line; this
                     includes the trades
  --book             print the final book after the statistics, best price first, then
                     the last trade price
  --validate MODE    check the book's invariants: none, final (the default), or
                     per-command (after every command; slower)
  --symbol SYM       let a v1 or v2 file stand in for this one instrument; a file that
                     names its own symbols refuses it
  --no-statistics    leave out the statistics
  --                 treat the next argument as the file, even if it starts with '-'
  -h, --help         print this help and exit

verify, checkpoint roll, and cut-log options, each given once, with no engine using the
directories, whose locks they take:
  --log-dir DIR         the engine's log directory; required
  --checkpoint-dir DIR  its checkpoint directory, if not the log directory
  --validate MODE       verify only: check each book once replayed, final (the default),
                        or after every command, per-command, which names a poison command
  --shard S, --position P
                        cut-log only, required: the shard, and the log position of the
                        first record to move, as recovery names it

send options, in any order before or after the file:
  --connect ADDR:PORT  the engine's IPv4 address and port; required
  --symbol SYM         as for replay, and required for a v1 or v2 file
  --                   as for replay

exit status:
  0  replayed to the end, even if the book rejected some commands; or help printed;
     for send, every command answered; for checkpoint, the file described or verified,
     or the store rolled; for verify, a start would pass, a torn tail it cuts included;
     for cut-log, the records moved
  1  input error: the file can't be opened or read, or is malformed (what ran before
     the error is still printed); for send, also no connection; for checkpoint, not a
     checkpoint file, or one verify refuses, or a checkpoint roll couldn't publish; for
     verify, a start would stop; for cut-log, refused; and for each store command, a
     directory another engine or command is using
  2  usage error
  3  engine failure: the book threw, or failed an invariant check; for verify, a book
     broke, named with its command under --validate per-command; for checkpoint roll, a
     rolled book failed its proof; for send, the
     connection was lost or the engine sent ProtocolError before every command was
     answered, so what ran is unknown (this wins over 1 and 4)
  4  send only: every command answered, but at least one was Refused, so that one
     certainly did not run and is safe to send again

A file that names instruments prints one section per symbol, sorted by symbol bytes,
each holding one book's output.

examples:
  order_book_cli replay orders.csv
  order_book_cli replay orders.csv --trades
  order_book_cli replay orders.csv --trades --book
  order_book_cli replay orders.csv --events --book --validate per-command
  order_book_cli send orders.csv --connect 127.0.0.1:9000 --symbol AAPL
